[33mcommit ffebf435b07b638af9ab3a561c175e80d16bb7c2[m
Author: Atin <ops@demo.lan>
Date:   Wed Oct 21 17:08:50 2026 -0700

    C11-468: Decide startup resume per panel; a failed or slow audit writes nothing (#708)
    
    * C11-468: Decide startup resume per panel; a failed or slow audit writes nothing
    
    After the 2026-10-21 reboot, c11 1.1.3 resumed no agent: the startup
    conversation audit held main for a fixed 5 s over a scrape of every agent
    panel, and on timeout markAllUnknown demoted all 32 refs, exact causal ones
    included. Autosave persisted the damage.
    
    - StartupConversationAudit replaces ResumeStartupEpochGate. Seed, classify
      (dirty transcript stats, no-resume policy) and one scrape per kind run as
      chained detached tasks; each restored panel awaits only the phase its own
      decision needs. Main waits for the in-memory seed alone (bounded at 1 s;
      a timeout is harmless), and autosave and the quit path await the seed.
    - No failure, timeout or late path writes state. markAllUnknown remains only
      for the app restart --no-resume policy. startupUnaudited is gone.
    - Scrape only panels whose ref is missing, a placeholder or inferred; Codex
      keeps its causal contexts for id reservation and the same-cwd rule. Shared
      with the quit path. Late-resolved panels resume only if untouched.
    - captureRuntimeEnv heals a same-id report on a ref that is not alive; seed
      heals causal refs that 1.1.3 demoted with "startup audit failed".
    - c11 conversation resume (--panel | --all-skipped) [--dry-run] [--json]
      reruns this launch's decision and types only into untouched shells.
    - One conversation.startup_audit event per launch with per-phase timings,
      the main-thread seed wait, panels by evidence tier and scrape failures.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * C11-468: Keep /exit sessions down, seed in memory, verify transcripts off the actor
    
    From the plan review:
    - Drop the seed heal of 1.1.3 damage. A Claude /exit lands as an `ended`
      push stored as `unknown`, and 1.1.3's markAllUnknown overwrote those too,
      so a healed ref could resurrect a session the operator ended.
    - The seed phase is in memory only (Codex launch-boundary markers move into
      classification), and every synchronous store reader that feeds a snapshot
      awaits it, so shutdown, power-off and window-unregister saves can never
      persist an unseeded store.
    - Dirty transcript checks run off the store actor and commit in one actor
      turn that skips any ref that changed meanwhile; one walk of
      ~/.codex/sessions answers every Codex ref. Socket conversation calls are no
      longer stalled behind Codex tree walks.
    - A late resume needs a shell idle at its prompt, or a terminal that has not
      started yet; an attached shell in an unknown state is not idle.
    - Restore-off launches leave the audit at its default, as the gate did.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * C11-468: One guarded submission for every resume; never persist a timed-out read
    
    From the first cross-family review (Astra):
    - Every resume decided this launch (restore, late scrape, conversation
      resume) is typed through Workspace.submitResume, which rechecks at typing
      time: a live writer elsewhere, a panel already resumed this launch, or a
      panel someone typed in since launch each become a skip. A refusal never
      downgrades a resumed outcome.
    - The synchronous store read reports a timeout instead of returning an empty
      map, and buildSessionSnapshot skips that save.
    - Dirty transcript checks for scrape-dependent refs run after their kind's
      scrape (success or failure), so a stale inferred ref is not restamped
      newer than the session the scrape finds.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    * C11-468: conversation resume reports scheduled; assert a second submission types nothing
    
    Review non-blocking items: the verb schedules typing through the guarded
    submission, which can still refuse, so its row says `scheduled`, not
    `resumed`. The guarded-submission test now checks the terminal's pending
    input, not just the outcome.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    
    ---------
    
    Co-authored-by: Atin <operator.at.work@example.com>
    Co-authored-by: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

